{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "{"id": 465, "depth": 0, "text": "Allah (; , , ) is the common Arabic word for God. In the English language, the word generally refers to God in Islam. The word is thought to be derived by contraction from al-ilāh, which means \"the god\", and is linguistically related to the Aramaic words Elah and Syriac  (ʼAlāhā) and the Hebrew word El (Elohim) for God.\n\nThe word Allah has been used by Arabic people of different religions since pre-Islamic times. The pre-Islamic Arabs worshipped a supreme deity whom they called Allah, alongside other lesser deities. Muhammad used the word Allah to indicate the Islamic conception of God. Allah has been used as a term for God by Muslims (both Arab and non-Arab), Judaeo-Arabic-speaking Jews, and Arab Christians after the term \"al-ilāh\" and \"Allah\" were used interchangeably in Classical Arabic by the majority of Arabs who had become Muslims. It is also often, albeit not exclusively, used in this way by Bábists, Baháʼís, Mandaeans, Indonesian and Maltese Christians, and Sephardi Jews, as well as by the Gagauz people. Similar usage by Christians", "seed_id": 93, "token_count": 256, "model": "qwen3-8b"}
{"id": 466, "depth": 1, "text": " and Jews with no relation to Arab cultures exists in other places such as Ethiopia, Sicily, Anatolia and Southern France.\n\nThe word \"Allah\" is an Arabic word meaning \"the God\" (al-ilāh), and is used in the Quran as an Arabic term for \"God\" in monotheistic reference to the God of Islam. In Arabic, the word Allah is always written with the initial alif, and the final letter is generally either a ha (a hamza being used in some Quranic manuscripts) or an hāʾ. In the latter case, the word is written الله. The term is pronounced [ʔalˤlah] (help·info) in Classical Arabic, with a short ā and a light /h/ sound, and [ʔalˤlah] (help·info) in Modern Standard Arabic, with a long ā and a heavy /h/ sound. In both cases, the alif is pronounced as a glottal stop [ʔ] and the hamza is pronounced as a glottal stop [ʔ] or as a voiceless pharyngeal fricative [ħ]. The alif can be pronounced as a glottal stop [ʔ] or a glottalized bil", "seed_id": 93, "token_count": 256, "model": "qwen3-8b"}
